<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Zayyanid Kingdom of Tlemcen</h1><p class="st-meta">1235 CE – 1556 CE · capital Tlemcen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Abdalwadid kingdom of Tlemcen, a Zenata Berber state that held the central Maghreb and a key terminus of the Saharan gold trade for three centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayyanid-dynasty">Banu Abd al-Wad</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanata-berbers">Zenata Berber</a> tribe, served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> power faded, their chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaghmurasan-ibn-zayyan">Yaghmurasan ibn Zayyan</a> took control of the city in 1235 and soon ruled independently, fending off both the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate</a> to the east and the rising <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/marinid-sultanate">Marinids</a> to the west.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> sat where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> reached the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oran">Oran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honaine">Honaine</a>, trading gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves for European cloth. The court patronised scholars, built mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, and hosted a large Jewish community. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-hammu-musa-ii">Abu Hammu Musa II</a> was himself a poet and author of a manual on kingship.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom endured repeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid</a> sieges and occupations, including 1337 to 1348 and 1352 to 1359. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-oran">Spanish Oran</a> from 1509 and the corsairs of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/regency-of-algiers">Regency of Algiers</a> then reduced it to a contested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> definitively in the 1550s.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/zayyanid-kingdom-of-tlemcen.jpg" alt="Zayyanid Kingdom of Tlemcen" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: M.Bitton · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zayyanid_Kingdom_at_the_beginning_of_the_14th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1235 CE – 1556 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tlemcen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yaghmurasan ibn Zayyan, Abu Tashufin I, Abu Hammu Musa II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Maliki)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zayyanids, Abdalwadids, Kingdom of Tlemcen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1396&amp;focus=zayyanid-kingdom-of-tlemcen" aria-label="Zayyanid Kingdom of Tlemcen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4647 915 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zayyanid Kingdom of Tlemcen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4967 1171L4794 1282M4967 1171L4873 1200M4967 1171L4910 989M4967 1171L5077 1108"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4794 1282h0M4873 1200h0M4910 989h0M5077 1108h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4967 1171h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4967 1171h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tlemcen. <a href="/atlas?year=1396&amp;focus=zayyanid-kingdom-of-tlemcen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
